<Base title="About" description="Who I am and what I work on.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>I grew up paying attention to signals before I knew they had a name. My first technical training was in signal processing, then GIS, then economics. The thread was always: how do you tell, from messy data, what's actually going on underneath. By the time I got to graduate school, this had narrowed into a specific obsession with the geography of capital flows. Who gets credit, where, on what terms. Most of my research is some version of that question.</p>

  <p>I finished my PhD at Georgia Tech in 2024. The dissertation was on network centrality, spatial heterogeneity, and optimal transport in international trade. It was not the kind of dissertation that ends up cited in policy briefs. But the methods turned out to travel. The way you decompose a trade-network shock into direct and indirect components is the same way you decompose a place-based-credit program into between-intermediary and within-intermediary effects. Same algebra, different setting. I've spent the time since finishing trying to make the connection useful.</p>

  <h2>The thing I'm doing right now</h2>

  <p>A working paper on the U.S. New Markets Tax Credit. The headline result is that the apparent rural mobilization gap, which has been a persistent talking point in CDFI Fund advocacy for a decade, is almost entirely between-CDE selection. Within any given CDE, rural projects don't leverage worse than urban ones in a way you can statistically detect. The penalty isn't a market problem. It's an allocation problem. That has direct implications for how the CDFI Fund should design its allocation rounds: send more rural-targeted dollars through high-leverage urban-experienced CDEs, with rural carve-outs, rather than subsidize rural projects more inside intermediaries that don't really do leverage. The paper is heading to SSRN in the next few weeks.</p>

  <p>I'm also rolling that methodology forward into the European setting, with <a href="/people/">Katia Antunes</a>, who's about to do fieldwork in Lisbon and Dublin. The Portuguese SGM structure (four mutual-guarantee societies, one of them agriculture-specialized) is a clean European analogue to the U.S. CDE structure. The Irish SBCI works through commercial-bank on-lenders, which is a different intermediary geometry. Comparing the two should let us see whether the intermediary-selection mechanism transfers across institutional designs, or whether the U.S. result is a U.S. artifact. Either answer is publishable.</p>

  <p>The third thread is joint work with <a href="/people/elizaveta-gonchar">Dr. Elizaveta Gonchar</a>, who's also my fiancée. We're building an effective-distance dataset (EffDist V2026) and have a paper out on SSRN, <em>Trade in the Spotlight</em>, applying it to bilateral exposure under sanctions. The metric replaces great-circle distance with shortest-network-path distance through a probability-weighted trade-flow graph. It's a small idea that does a surprising amount of work in a gravity equation.</p>

  <h2>The teaching</h2>

  <p>I run three teaching sites because they keep me honest about my research. If I can't explain a method clearly enough for a competent undergraduate to use it on their own data, I probably don't understand the method as well as I think I do. The biggest of these is <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/inference-lab/">Inference Lab</a>, which has 15 chapters on the methods I actually use, with R and Stata code side by side. <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/macroprep/">Macro Prep</a> is a reference site for intermediate macro with live FRED dashboards. <a href="https://github.com/ihelfrich/pictures-of-inference">Pictures of Inference</a> is a slow-cooking book on visual statistics, drafted in Quarto.</p>

  <p>The tutoring is one-to-one work with graduate students and advanced undergraduates. I take maybe four or five clients at a time, mostly people working on applied-causal projects in development or finance. If you're trying to figure out whether your data supports an RDD or whether you need to fall back to a fuzzy design, that's the kind of conversation I'm useful for. If you want someone to do your problem sets, I'm not.</p>

  <h2>What I don't do</h2>

  <p>I don't ghostwrite. I don't sign onto papers I haven't substantively contributed to. I don't take consulting engagements whose conclusion is already decided. I don't post on Twitter (the account is dormant; Bluesky is the only social I check). I don't currently take big institutional retainer-style work because I'd rather finish the paper.</p>

  <h2>Where I live</h2>

  <p>Atlanta. I work mostly from home, occasionally from the library at Tech, and I travel for conferences and fieldwork. The next trip is NEUDC in November if the submission lands. Before that, a visit to Lisbon with Katia. <a href="/now">More on the rolling week-by-week.</a></p>

